## 1. Strategic Decision: PWA vs Flutter

Before committing, here is an honest comparison for your use case:

| Dimension | Current PWA | Flutter Native |
|-----------|-------------|----------------|
| **Distribution** | URL — zero install | TestFlight / APK sideload |
| **Offline Storage** | IndexedDB (Dexie.js) — 50-250 MB limit, browser-dependent | SQLite / Hive — virtually unlimited, OS-level |
| **Camera / GPS** | Web APIs (fragmented on iOS Safari) | Native plugins — reliable, consistent |
| **Push Notifications** | Web Push (broken on iOS <16.4, unreliable) | FCM/APNs — reliable, native |
| **Performance** | JS engine, browser overhead | AOT-compiled Dart, ~60fps |
| **Background Sync** | Service Worker (no iOS background fetch) | `workmanager` plugin, native background tasks |
| **Auto-Update** | Instant (SW update) | TestFlight auto-update or manual |
| **Code Reuse** | ❌ Full rewrite | ❌ Full rewrite (different language/framework) |
| **Codebase Size** | ~50K LoC React/TS | Estimated ~40-50K LoC Dart |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Architect's Verdict**: Flutter is a strong upgrade for your use case. The biggest wins are **reliable offline storage** (SQLite ≫ IndexedDB), **native camera/GPS**, and **true background sync on iOS**. These are exactly the pain points of a field audit app. The trade-off is a **full rewrite** — no code can be directly ported.

