## 4. Offline-First Parity Strategy

Your PWA has 8 battle-tested offline phases. Here's the Flutter equivalent:

| PWA Phase | Flutter Equivalent | Package |
|-----------|-------------------|---------|
| **Phase 0: Dexie.js** | **Drift** (type-safe SQLite ORM) | `drift` + `sqlite3_flutter_libs` |
| **Phase 1: TanStack Persister** | **Riverpod + Drift cache** | Custom cache layer |
| **Phase 2: Auth Persistence** | **Supabase `SharedPreferences`** | `supabase_flutter` (built-in session persistence) |
| **Phase 3: Photo Storage** | **SQLite blobs or file system** | `path_provider` + `dart:io` |
| **Phase 4: Transactional Outbox** | **Drift outbox table** | Custom `OutboxRepository` |
| **Phase 5: Sync Engine** | **Dart Isolate + `connectivity_plus`** | Background processing in isolate |
| **Phase 6: App Shell** | **Native — not needed** | Flutter is natively cached |
| **Phase 7: Mutation Integration** | **Same outbox pattern** | Riverpod mutations → Drift outbox |
| **Phase 8: Auto-Update** | **TestFlight / CodePush** | `shorebird` for OTA updates |

### Key Architectural Decisions

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[User Submits Audit] --> B{Online?}
    B -->|Yes| C[POST to Supabase]
    B -->|No| D[Write to Drift Outbox]
    D --> E[Show in Pending Queue UI]
    E --> F{Connectivity Restored?}
    F -->|Yes| G[Sync Engine processes FIFO]
    G --> C
    F -->|No| E
    C --> H[Invalidate Riverpod Cache]
```

> [!TIP]
> **Flutter Advantage**: Unlike the PWA where iOS Safari kills Service Workers aggressively, Flutter's `workmanager` plugin provides real background sync on both iOS and Android.

